    {
      "source": "hn",
      "title": "Show HN: Get your coding agent off ~2019 JavaScript, onto modern web primitives",
      "url": "https://github.com/GoogleChrome/modern-web-guidance",
      "author": "paulirish",
      "external_id": "50443812",
      "summary": null,
      "why_read": null,
      "authors": [],
      "category": null,
      "published": "2026-11-10T18:01:15Z",
      "score": 410,
      "comments": 134,
      "hn_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50443812",
      "snippet": "Browsers natively handle a lot of what used to take 50 lines of JS (or a dependency): `CSS.highlights` for text range highlighting without &lt;span&gt;s everywhere, `&lt;dialog&gt;` and showModal() give you backdrops and ideal focus management, `field-sizing` to auto-grow textareas as the user types.. (Something yesterday&#x27;s ftrain post touched on [0])<p>Models know about some of these by name, but consistently won&#x27;t default to using the platform-native solution and instead prefer the incumbent &lt;div&gt;+JS based pattern.  We started an effort in Chrome to supplement coding agents with this key domain knowledge and we&#x27;re shipping our 1.0 today, in collaboration with Firefox, MDN, and Edge.<p>200+ guides (~1.7MB) of curated, hand-crafted implementation best-practices from web experts, updated as the web evolves.  (Open source, obv, so.. we&#x27;ll welcome your bikeshedding",
      "links": []
    },
